1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:03,415
Here's the failure mode this lecture exists to prevent.

2
00:00:03,415 --> 00:00:07,706
An ad set is running, the numbers are mediocre, not clearly dead, not clearly

3
00:00:07,706 --> 00:00:10,659
working, and someone decides to give it one more day.

4
00:00:10,659 --> 00:00:11,384
Then another.

5
00:00:11,384 --> 00:00:12,498
That's not patience.

6
00:00:12,498 --> 00:00:17,068
That's a decision being made by hope instead of by a rule, and hope has never once

7
00:00:17,068 --> 00:00:18,907
correctly identified a losing ad.

8
00:00:18,907 --> 00:00:22,362
You've actually seen this trap before, from a different angle.

9
00:00:22,362 --> 00:00:26,987
Section 5.5 warned you that a platform's own automation optimises whatever you hand

10
00:00:26,987 --> 00:00:29,773
it without judging whether it deserved optimising.

11
00:00:29,773 --> 00:00:34,454
An unwilling-to-kill instinct is the human version of that same blind spot: no rule,

12
00:00:34,454 --> 00:00:37,074
no judgement about when to stop, just momentum.

13
00:00:37,074 --> 00:00:41,699
The fix is deciding the numbers before the test starts, while you have no emotional

14
00:00:41,699 --> 00:00:43,928
stake in any single ad set's result yet.

15
00:00:43,928 --> 00:00:46,101
Harlan's account runs on exact figures.

16
00:00:46,101 --> 00:00:50,504
Yours should too, built from your own margin the same way Section 1.5 built the

17
00:00:50,504 --> 00:00:52,788
twenty-four dollar ceiling from Harlan's.

18
00:00:52,788 --> 00:00:56,522
Why does this need to happen before the data arrives, specifically?

19
00:00:56,522 --> 00:00:59,754
Because a number chosen mid-test is never really a number.

20
00:00:59,754 --> 00:01:00,423
It's a mood.

21
00:01:00,423 --> 00:01:04,770
Watch a mediocre result for three days and your sense of "good enough" quietly

22
00:01:04,770 --> 00:01:07,389
drifts to match whatever you're already seeing.

23
00:01:07,939 --> 00:01:12,616
Eight ad sets, twenty-five dollars a day each, run against a twenty-four-dollar CAC

24
00:01:12,616 --> 00:01:16,504
ceiling that Section 1.5 already worked out from Harlan's own margin.

25
00:01:16,504 --> 00:01:17,969
None of that is arbitrary.

26
00:01:17,969 --> 00:01:22,477
Every number traces back to something you've already seen this course establish.

27
00:01:22,477 --> 00:01:26,139
Notice, too, that eight isn't a round number chosen for tidiness.

28
00:01:26,139 --> 00:01:30,873
It's however many of the strongest angles from the angle map genuinely earned a slot

29
00:01:30,873 --> 00:01:35,549
in this first test, not padded up to a nicer-looking figure and not trimmed down to

30
00:01:35,549 --> 00:01:36,226
save budget.

31
00:01:36,226 --> 00:01:40,903
The number of ad sets should follow from the strength of your angles, not the other

32
00:01:40,903 --> 00:01:41,466
way round.

33
00:01:41,466 --> 00:01:45,185
Notice, too, how the daily budget interacts with the ad-set count.

34
00:01:45,185 --> 00:01:49,918
Twenty-five dollars a day across eight ad sets is two hundred dollars a day total, a

35
00:01:49,918 --> 00:01:53,412
real number worth looking at honestly before you commit to it.

36
00:01:53,412 --> 00:01:56,736
If that figure makes you flinch, that's useful information.

37
00:01:56,736 --> 00:02:01,301
It means either the angle count needs trimming to the genuinely strongest few, or

38
00:02:01,301 --> 00:02:05,865
the per-set budget needs to come down until the total feels like a number you can

39
00:02:05,865 --> 00:02:08,513
actually sustain for the length of a real test.

40
00:02:09,063 --> 00:02:11,913
Here's the exact moment a kill rule earns its keep.

41
00:02:11,913 --> 00:02:16,494
Without one, "give it another day" sounds reasonable every single time you say it,

42
00:02:16,494 --> 00:02:19,678
because it always feels premature to call something dead.

43
00:02:19,678 --> 00:02:23,086
With the rule already decided, there's nothing to relitigate.

44
00:02:23,086 --> 00:02:27,444
Fifty dollars, zero add-to-carts, finished, and that budget moves to an ad set

45
00:02:27,444 --> 00:02:29,566
that's actually telling you something.

46
00:02:29,566 --> 00:02:33,756
Notice why zero add-to-carts specifically, rather than a softer metric like

47
00:02:33,756 --> 00:02:34,818
click-through rate.

48
00:02:34,818 --> 00:02:39,343
An add-to-cart is someone doing something, not just seeing something, and it sits

49
00:02:39,343 --> 00:02:43,198
closer to the outcome you actually care about than a click ever does.

50
00:02:43,198 --> 00:02:46,662
Fifty dollars with genuinely zero of those isn't a slow start.

51
00:02:46,662 --> 00:02:48,505
It's an angle that isn't landing.

52
00:02:48,505 --> 00:02:51,019
Notice the rule isn't harsh for its own sake.

53
00:02:51,019 --> 00:02:55,433
It's specifically calibrated to Harlan's twenty-five-dollar daily budget: fifty

54
00:02:55,433 --> 00:03:00,070
dollars is two days' spend, enough to see a genuine signal, not so much that a dead

55
00:03:00,070 --> 00:03:02,975
ad set burns a week of budget before anyone notices.

56
00:03:02,975 --> 00:03:06,830
Ask yourself what the equivalent number is for your own daily budget.

57
00:03:06,830 --> 00:03:11,467
The ratio matters more than the specific figure: roughly two days' spend before you

58
00:03:11,467 --> 00:03:16,048
call it, calibrated so a genuinely dead angle doesn't quietly drain a week's worth

59
00:03:16,048 --> 00:03:19,232
of budget while everyone assumes it just needs more time.

60
00:03:19,782 --> 00:03:23,565
One honest limit before you copy any of this onto your own account.

61
00:03:23,565 --> 00:03:27,970
These specific figures exist because of Harlan's specific contribution margin.

62
00:03:27,970 --> 00:03:32,487
Your CAC ceiling comes from your own economics, the same calculation Section 1.5

63
00:03:32,487 --> 00:03:37,174
walked through, not from borrowing Harlan's number because it's the one this course

64
00:03:37,174 --> 00:03:38,868
happened to use as an example.

65
00:03:38,868 --> 00:03:42,708
Redo that maths for your own product before you set a single budget.

66
00:03:42,708 --> 00:03:47,338
Notice the same warning applies to the daily budget and the ad-set count, not just

67
00:03:47,338 --> 00:03:48,242
the CAC ceiling.

68
00:03:48,242 --> 00:03:52,929
Twenty-five dollars a day made sense against Harlan's total daily test spend of two

69
00:03:52,929 --> 00:03:57,615
hundred dollars, itself a number sized to what her business could actually sustain.

70
00:03:57,615 --> 00:04:02,302
Your version of that figure depends entirely on your own cash flow, not on matching

71
00:04:02,302 --> 00:04:04,504
someone else's comfort level with risk.

72
00:04:04,504 --> 00:04:06,707
This isn't a small caveat to skip past.

73
00:04:06,707 --> 00:04:11,393
A CAC ceiling copied from a product with a different margin structure is worse than

74
00:04:11,393 --> 00:04:15,967
no ceiling at all, because it looks precise and rigorous while actually measuring

75
00:04:15,967 --> 00:04:17,774
nothing about your own business.

76
00:04:17,774 --> 00:04:22,405
Precision borrowed from someone else's numbers is just a confident-sounding guess.

77
00:04:22,955 --> 00:04:27,398
Next lecture solves a quieter problem: once you're running eight ad sets a week,

78
00:04:27,398 --> 00:04:31,676
every week, how do you actually find the one that worked six months from now,

79
00:04:31,676 --> 00:04:35,564
instead of scrolling through a library of files nobody can tell apart.

80
00:04:35,564 --> 00:04:40,174
Before that, write your own two numbers down now, today, not once a test is already

81
00:04:40,174 --> 00:04:40,452
live.

82
00:04:40,452 --> 00:04:43,229
Your CAC ceiling, worked out from your own margin.

83
00:04:43,229 --> 00:04:47,895
And your kill rule, roughly two days' spend with zero of whatever behavioural signal

84
00:04:47,895 --> 00:04:50,173
actually matters for what you're selling.

85
00:04:50,173 --> 00:04:54,117
Decide both while you have no result yet to talk you out of either one.
